	"identifying_description": ".1 - Roof wheel, double rim enclosing a central cross, 2 groups of 10 curved poles bound together with leather.2.1-59 - Roof struts --fifty-nine.3.1-4 -  Wall lattices, accordian-jointed - four.4 - Door frame, wood and wrought iron, wood jambs - WARE001F00, 64 x 35 1/2 x 120\".5.1-2 - Upper and lower door crossbars, wood with five holes (upper) and four sockets (lower).6.1-2 - Right and left entrance doors.7 -  Ram's kkull.8.1-2 - Cane screens for exterior - two.9.1-2 - Roof felts, semi-circular, lined with white canvas and red cotton - two.10.1-4 - Wall felts, lined with white canvas and red cotton - four.11 - Tent band, wool with red and black center stripe, black, red, brown outer stripe; large.12.1-12 - Tent bands, various colors and designs, small - twelve.13 - Cord ties, many dark brown goat hair tie cords.",
	"web_text": "The Turkmen people of Central Asia have traditionally lived in round, felt-covered dwellings that can be disassembled and reassembled elsewhere. This allows for regular migration between summer and winter pastures, which is necessary for the grazing of sheep, goats, and camels. An ak öý (white dwelling) differs from the typical Turkmen gara öý (black dwelling). For an ak öý, the felt is made from white wool, which is less common than black wool. The ak öý is typically the home of newlyweds.",
